The sample is from the test directory of tar package release.
Here is just for quick reference.

Build (from within an extracted release package, so that ../include and ../lib
resolve):

    make

Each .cpp builds into its own executable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Media samples (video / audio / subtitle / extension data)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  write_sample_code       Writer example.
  read_sample_code        Reader example.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MPEG-TS samples (tvutsshm)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Transport of a muxed MPEG transport stream over shared memory. TS bytes are
carried in the user data field of each item, tagged with
LIBSHM_MEDIA_TYPE_MPEG_TS_DATA ('TMTS', 0x53544D54). See section 8.5 of
doc/MediaMesh_Shared_Memory_SDK.md for the full contract.

  mpegts_write_sample_code    Feed a TS file or stdin into a segment, with
                              optional constant-bitrate pacing. -r varies the
                              packet count per item (still whole packets) to
                              check that a consumer does not hardcode a size.
  mpegts_read_sample_code     Attach to a segment, validate TS structure (sync
                              bytes, PIDs, continuity counters), detect
                              reader-side data loss, and optionally write the
                              recovered stream to a file or stdout.
  make_test_ts.py             Generate a synthetic TS with strictly correct
                              continuity counters, for proving out an
                              integration before using real content.

Quick round trip:

    ./make_test_ts.py sample.ts
    ./mpegts_read_sample_code  -n LiveTransmit -v          # terminal 1
    ./mpegts_write_sample_code -n LiveTransmit -i sample.ts -b 8000000 -l

Pipe the recovered stream into other tooling:

    ./mpegts_read_sample_code -n LiveTransmit -o - -q | ffprobe -

Run either binary with -h for the full option list.

IMPORTANT: the writer never blocks. A reader that consumes slower than the
writer produces will silently lose data, and that loss looks exactly like
continuity counter errors in the recovered stream. mpegts_read_sample_code
reports the read/write index lag so reader-side loss can be told apart from
genuine stream defects. Keep your read loop thin and do analysis on another
thread.
